1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,012
The Power of Music Across Cultures.

2
00:00:02,962 --> 00:00:06,412
Music exists in every known human culture.

3
00:00:06,412 --> 00:00:11,825
Bone flutes over 40,000 years old predate agriculture and permanent settlements.

4
00:00:11,825 --> 00:00:20,362
Across societies, music serves consistent functions: strengthening community bonds, expressing emotion, and accompanying ritual.

5
00:00:20,362 --> 00:00:31,337
Universal patterns recur: nearly all cultures employ octave intervals; major tonalities tend to sound bright and minor ones somber, a pattern linked to vocal acoustics.

6
00:00:31,337 --> 00:00:35,525
Music activates dopamine release in the brain's reward system.

7
00:00:35,525 --> 00:00:42,450
Group music-making synchronizes performers' neural oscillations, creating measurable physiological alignment.

8
00:00:42,450 --> 00:00:51,400
These findings suggest music preceded language as a social bonding mechanism, enabling emotional communication before complex speech developed.

9
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:57,337
The universality of music across all known societies points to its deep evolutionary roots.

